ormatting, persistent configuration, and the reboot were causally necessary. The controlled unmount/close/reopen cycle, incorrect-key test, health marker, and post-reboot write test were bounded verification actions; their temporary artifacts were removed. Restricted rollback copies and the health marker remain, but both have clear recovery or persistence-verification roles and are root-only. Final evidence shows no failed units, no unexpected listener changes, healthy package management, and no executor temporary-file residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-978c1ed8ff714510","cmd-67589bfff4e340aa","cmd-c9ea6ee26a9f46e4","cmd-b7568100806a43c4","cmd-96ae2d3c6b894289","cmd-19bd187506ba4dcc","cmd-9debd02a19394169","cmd-5d9a4233543f4785","cmd-2a5f74ef8bed4198","cmd-c49c3c67040449e0","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 has a working LUKS2-backed XFS volume at /srv/secure, and a directly observed reboot transition proves automatic unlock, mount, data preservation, and write availability. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete and agrees with the fixed lifecycle boot identities. The operating-system release and kernel were preserved.","limitations":["The backing container is file-based and its root-only unlock key resides on the root filesystem, so the evidence establishes encrypted-volume behavior but not protection against compromise of the running host or its root filesystem.","The plaintext-at-rest check searched for one known marker; it is supportive rather than an exhaustive cryptographic audit."]}
